'''acerdese''' (as′ėr-dēs), ''n.'' [F.] Gray oxid of manganese: a name given by Beudant to the mineral manganite.

'''acere''' (as′ēr), ''n.'' A mollusk of the genus ''Acera.''

'''aceric''' (a-ser′ik), ''a.'' [< L. ''acer'' (see ''Acer'') + ''-ic.''] Pertaining to the maple; obtained from the maple.—{{smaller|'''Aceric acid''', an acid found in the juice of ''Acer campestre'', the common European maple.}}

'''Acerina''' (as-e-rī′nä), ''n.'' [NL., as ''Acerus'', q. v., + ''-ina.''] '''1.''' A genus of crustaceans. ''Rafinesque'', 1814.—'''2.''' A genus of percoid fishes, the popes. ''Cuvier'', 1817.

'''Acerineæ''' (as-e-rin′ẹ̄-ē), ''n. pl.'' [< ''Acer'' + ''-in-'' + ''-eæ.''] A suborder of the ''Sapindaceæ'', distinguished from the rest of the order by its opposite leaves and exalbuminous seeds. {{smaller|It includes the maple (''Acer''), the box-elder (''Negundo''), and a third genus, ''Dobinea'', of a single species, native of the Himalayas.}}

'''Acerininæ''' (as″e-ri-nī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [< ''Acerina'', 2, + ''-inæ.''] A name proposed as a subfamily designation for the genus ''Acerina'', including the ruffe and related percoid fishes having a cavernous head and a single dorsal fin.
